<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Olmsted is a 73-year-old man with mild dementia of undetermined type (CDR 1), on donepezil 5 mg each morning for six weeks, who comes for a same-day visit with his caregiver. Five days ago he developed a runny nose and a scratchy throat, followed by a dry cough. Two nights ago, to help him sleep through the cough, his caregiver gave him an over-the-counter nighttime cold medicine containing diphenhydramine 25 mg with acetaminophen, and gave it again last night. On both nights he woke after midnight, could not find the bathroom, and was briefly unsure where he was; he settled after about half an hour with reassurance and the lights on. During the day he has been at his usual level, by his caregiver&apos;s account. He has had no fever above 37.6 °C at home, no breathlessness, no chest pain, no urinary symptoms, no falls, and no new medicines other than the cold medicine. He has not missed a donepezil dose and has had no nausea or diarrhea. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Five days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Runny nose, scratchy throat, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild; cough disturbs sleep</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Night-time confusion on the two nights he took the diphenhydramine product; at baseline in the daytime</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Mild dementia; donepezil 5 mg daily; nighttime cold medicine given for sleep</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Settled with reassurance and the lights on</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No fever above 37.6 °C, breathlessness, chest pain, urinary symptoms, headache, weakness, or falls</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

