    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Common cold, with night-time confusion after diphenhydramine (J00)</content>. Viral upper respiratory infection with a clear chest, normal oxygen saturation, and no fever, so pneumonia is not suspected and antibiotics are not indicated. The night-time confusion began with a strongly anticholinergic antihistamine, which also works against donepezil. He has no delirium now (4AT 1, from his baseline disorientation to the year), no urinary retention, and no sign of another infection, so no tests are needed today. [S]<list><item>Stop the nighttime cold medicine, and avoid all products containing diphenhydramine, doxylamine, or chlorpheniramine, including over-the-counter sleep aids labeled PM.</item><item>Supportive care: honey in warm drinks, fluids, saline nasal spray, and a humidifier.</item><item>Acetaminophen 650 mg up to every 6 hours as needed for sore throat, no more than 3 g a day.</item><item>Night lights on the way to the bathroom, and a regular bedtime routine.</item><item>The cough may last two to three weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Mild dementia, type undetermined (F03.A0)</content>. Daytime cognition at his baseline. Acute illness and anticholinergic drugs raise the risk of delirium in dementia; his caregiver was given the warning signs. [S]<list><item>Continue donepezil 5 mg each morning; the planned dose review stays at the three-month visit.</item><item>Call the same day for confusion in the daytime, drowsiness, fever above 38 °C, breathlessness, a fall, or trouble passing urine.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Non-Alzheimer&apos;s dementia and Alzheimer&apos;s disease monographs (2026); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in two days. Three-month dementia review as planned in three weeks. Next scheduled visit 1895-08-04: three-month dementia review: donepezil, safety plan, caregiver burden, and advance care planning. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
